    description: "[REVIEW 2026-11-01, CURRENT] Re-checked 2026-11-01 at dev 100b6fbb6e: rust.py still splits only _RUST_ABSOLUTE_PATH_ROOTS={std,core,alloc}. Fixture with Cargo.toml deps serde_json/tokio: `let r = serde_json::from_slice(..)` emits rust:external:0-0:serde_json..from_slice with dst_ref None (premise holds). But the item's own examples serde_json::from_str and tokio::fs::read emit NO edge at all: from_str/read are in SUPPRESSED_METHOD_NAMES['rust'] (INV-polad, 91a5b31813, 2026-08-24) and the guard drops an unbound denylisted leaf. A manifest-aware split would rescue those too (ext_ref set bypasses the guard). (Original text below; the review entry in the thread has the evidence.)\n\nFOUND 2026-09-26, INV-duzom's residual (PR #1229). A fully-qualified call through a THIRD-PARTY crate with no `use` statement, e.g. `serde_json::from_str(s)` or `tokio::fs::read(p)` (edition-2018 extern crates are in scope by name), still keeps the whole path in the NAME slot with the external sentinel module: rust:external:0-0:serde_json::from_str. INV-duzom splits only paths rooted at std/core/alloc (or a leading ::), because at the analyzer's external-call arm a crate name cannot be told from a local module name without the manifest, and a local `utils::helper` split into module `utils` would be a relative path posing as an owner.\n\nWHAT IT NEEDS: the crate's declared dependency names (Cargo.toml [dependencies], plus renames `package = ...`, plus workspace inheritance) available to the rust analyzer, so a head that names a dependency is absolute. Frequency NOT measured; the tokio::fs / serde_json shapes are the likely I/O-relevant ones."  # b61c
